WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
Calling a turbo module that hasn't been registered in your app will trigger a fatal system crash.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.920
Preventing this is complicated by a counter-intuitive gap in the framework.

3
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.720
The turbo module registry does not include a built-in is available method.

4
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.520
To validate a module safely, we check if retrieving it returns a null value.

5
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.400
To avoid evaluating this on every render, we place this check at the module scope.

6
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.400
Assigned to a constant, the result caches once during file import.

7
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:33.600
Caching this check up front is the only reliable way to guarantee baseline crash protection

8
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.800
without constantly taxing the UI rendering cycle.

9
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.840
This split-screen diagram illustrates co-location.

10
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.080
On the left, the specification file evaluates the availability constant,

11
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.920
exporting the Boolean outward.

12
00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.120
On the right, a consumer-react component imports it.

13
00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.440
If missing, a conditional statement safely routes the interface to a fallback state.

14
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.840
Establishing this structural co-location

15
00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.480
prevents the underlying module logic from polluting consumer UI components.

16
00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.840
A complication rises when a turbo module is engineered strictly for a single operating system,

17
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:07.920
like an Android-only feature.

18
00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.960
Placing OS-specific checks inside a consumer component

19
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.680
clutters the render cycle with platform logic.

20
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.360
We correct this by absorbing OS validation entirely into the spec files availability export.

21
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.240
The spec absorbs the complexity, returning the clean, single Boolean back to the interface.

22
00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.480
A UI component only needs to know if a module is ready to execute.

23
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:32.880
Keeping it completely blind to the technical reasons why a module might be disabled makes the code

24
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.120
base predictable. Consider a hardware-dependent module, like a flashlight,

25
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:41.840
existing in the app's code while running on a tablet that lacks the actual physical hardware.

26
00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.360
This diagram illustrates the two-stage validation required.

27
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:51.040
Gate 1 represents the registry check, confirming the module exists in software.

28
00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.720
Gate 2 is the capability check for physical hardware.

29
00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:00.320
The app verifies software availability first before querying the asynchronous hardware status.

30
00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.920
Relying solely on software registry checks for hardware modules leaves the application

31
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.960
vulnerable to physical device limitations. This manages the immediate software check

32
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:16.800
and the delayed hardware response simultaneously. Both logic blocks are enveloped inside a custom

33
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:23.040
react hook. This structure consolidates the multi-step validations into a single output pipeline,

34
00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:29.120
passing a unified ready state to the UI component. This level of encapsulation provides maximum

35
00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.400
safety for the application. It hides the complex validation sequence from front-end components,

36
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.960
letting developers safely interact with native features.

37
00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.400
This structured checklist maps the four core rules for implementing availability guards.

38
00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:50.400
First, execute the null check. Second, cache it at the module scope. Third, hide platform logic within

39
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:56.880
the spec file, and fourth, utilize two level checks for hardware features, applying these precise

40
00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:02.240
availability patterns ensures universal crash protection and architectural cleanliness across

41
00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.240
any device.

